.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt DRM-SYNCOBJ 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm drm-syncobj ,
.Nm syncobj ,
.Nm drm_syncobj
.Nd sync objects on a DRM card or render node
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A sync object holds a fence of
.Xr dma-buf 7 :
none, one fence, or, on a timeline, a chain of numbered points, each of
which signals once its own fence and every earlier point's have.
A program creates one on a card or render node of
.Xr drm 4 ,
names it by a handle of its open file, waits on it, signals or resets it,
moves fences between sync objects, and shares it with other files and
processes as a descriptor, or its fence as a sync file.
The structures and numbers are those of
.In drm/drm.h
in Linux 6.12.
.Pp
The calls answer only where the driver offers sync objects, as
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_GET_CAP
reports them:
.Dv DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ
for the binary calls and
.Dv DRM_CAP_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE
for
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TRANSFER ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_EVENTFD ,
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_SIGNAL
and
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_QUERY .
virtio_gpu and i915 offer both; bochs-drm, simpledrm and vkms neither.
.Ss Points
Point 0 names a sync object's newest fence, and a binary sync object has
no other.
A point added to a timeline continues it when it is above the newest;
one at or below the newest is numbered as the newest and starts a
timeline of its own, which hides the earlier points from
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_QUERY
until it signals.
Point
.Ar N
is covered by the newest point at or above
.Ar N
whose predecessor on its timeline is below
.Ar N ;
a wait for
.Ar N
waits for that point.
Points that have signalled are dropped as later ones arrive.
.Ss Calls
.Bl -tag -width DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_SIGNAL
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_CREATE
A new sync object in
.Ar handle ,
the lowest free from 1, signalled at once with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_CREATE_SIGNALED .
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_DESTROY
Drop a handle; the object lives while any handle, descriptor or waiter
holds it.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD
A close-on-exec descriptor of the object, shown as
.Ql anon_inode:syncobj_file ;
with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD_FLAGS_EXPORT_SYNC_FILE ,
a sync file of its newest fence instead.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_FD_TO_HANDLE
A handle to the object of such a descriptor, from any device; with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_FD_TO_HANDLE_FLAGS_IMPORT_SYNC_FILE ,
the fence of the sync file in
.Ar fd
becomes the fence of the object in
.Ar handle .
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_WAIT
Wait for the newest fence of each of
.Ar count_handles
objects to signal: any one, or all with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_ALL ,
until the absolute
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
time
.Ar timeout_nsec
in nanoseconds, where 0 or a time past asks once.
An object without a fence is
.Er EINVAL ,
unless
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_FOR_SUBMIT
waits for one to be given first.
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_DEADLINE
passes
.Ar deadline_nsec
to the fences as a hint.
.Ar first_signaled
is the index of the first object found signalled.
A fence's error does not fail the wait; a sync file of the fence reports
it.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT
The same at the points in
.Ar points ,
and with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_AVAILABLE
only until each point is submitted.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_RESET
Take each object's fence away.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_SIGNAL
Give each object a signalled fence.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_SIGNAL
Add a signalled point to each object at its point in
.Ar points ,
or for point 0 give it a signalled fence.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_QUERY
Write the point each object has reached into
.Ar points ,
0 for a binary object; with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_QUERY_FLAGS_LAST_SUBMITTED ,
its newest point.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_TRANSFER
Give the fence of
.Ar src_point
of one object to
.Ar dst_point
of another, or make it the other's fence for point 0;
.Ar flags
may hold
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_FOR_SUBMIT ,
which waits up to five seconds for the source point.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_EVENTFD
Signal the
.Xr eventfd 2
.Ar fd
once
.Ar point
signals, or with
.Dv DRM_SYNCOBJ_WAIT_FLAGS_WAIT_AVAILABLE
once it is submitted.
.El
.Pp
Each array call takes
.Ar count_handles
handles at the address
.Ar handles .
Padding and unused flags must be zero.
.Ss Errors
.Bl -tag -width EOPNOTSUPP
.It Er EFAULT
A bad address of handles or points.
.It Er EINVAL
No handles, an unknown flag or nonzero padding; a point not yet
submitted; a handle
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_DESTROY
or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD
does not know; a descriptor that is no sync object or sync file.
.It Er EINTR
A signal ended a wait.
.It Er ENOENT
A handle of no sync object, in every other call.
.It Er ENOMEM
No memory.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
A driver without the feature.
.It Er ETIME
The wait's time passed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr dma-buf 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The calls, structures, errors and the numbering of points are Linux
6.12's.
